## Behavioural rules carried over unchanged

1. **One JSONL file per `Logger` instance,** written to
   `<config.dir>/sessions/<session-id>.jsonl` by default (auto-generated
   session id: `YYYYMMDDTHHMMSSZ-<8 hex chars>`), or to an explicit
   `dir:`/`log:` override. The directory is created if missing. Every write
   is `flush`ed immediately (no buffering — a crash mid-run shouldn't lose
   log lines).
2. **Every log line is a flat JSON object** with `session_id`, `at` (ISO
   8601 timestamp), `phase`, plus phase-specific fields — never nested
   under a wrapper key. Phases: `session_start`, `iteration`,
   `limit_reached`, `turn_end`, `prompt`, `tool_call`, `tool_result`,
   `response`, `raw`.
3. **`raw` events are gated on `Boukensha.debug?`,** not on any
   per-`Logger`-instance flag — checked globally at call time inside
   `Logger#raw`, so toggling `Boukensha.debug!` mid-run changes behaviour
   for every already-constructed `Logger`/`Agent`.
4. **Tool-dispatch errors are now caught, not propagated.** This is the one
   genuine behavioural change to the agent loop itself in this step:
   `handle_tool_calls` wraps `@registry.dispatch(name, args)` in a
   `rescue StandardError => e`, turning any exception into the string
   `"ERROR: #{e.class}: #{e.message}"`, which becomes the `tool_result`
   content sent back to the model (so the agent can see the failure and
   retry/adapt) — logged via `tool_result(ok: false, error: e.message)`
   instead of crashing the whole `Agent#run` call. Before this step, an
   exception inside a tool's block would propagate all the way up and kill
   the loop.
5. **`Logger#response`'s `execution_metadata`** derives `provider` from
   `backend.class.name.split("::").last` converted from CamelCase to
   snake_case (e.g. `OllamaCloud` → `"ollama_cloud"`), reads `model` off
   `backend.model`, and computes `usage_unit`/`usage_level` only if the
   backend `respond_to?` them (only Ollama Cloud defines `usage_level`).
   `cost_usd` comes from `backend.estimate_cost(input_tokens:,
   output_tokens:)` when both token counts are present, else `nil`. The
   whole metadata hash is `.compact`-ed (Ruby: drops `nil` values) before
   merging into the log line.
6. **`Logger#normalized_usage`** reads whichever of `response["usage"]`
   (Anthropic/OpenAI), `response["usageMetadata"]` (Gemini), or Ollama's
   flat `prompt_eval_count`/`eval_count` top-level keys are present, in
   that priority order, returning `None`/`nil` if none exist. This is
   distinct from (and simpler than) `Logger#usage_tokens`'s per-key lookup
   used by `execution_metadata` — the two do overlap in purpose but are
   separate methods with separate call sites (`normalized_usage` feeds
   `log_response`'s `usage:` kwarg; `usage_tokens` is used internally by
   `execution_metadata`) — port both as separate methods, not unified.
7. **`Logger#subscribe`(this step doesn't have it yet — see next
   plan's note)** — not present in `06_the_logger`; do not port it here
   (it's a `07_the_run_dsl` addition, listed in that step's plan).
8. **The `Agent`'s reasoning-text-before-tool-calls log line** falls back
   to a synthesized placeholder (`"(tool use — N call(s))"`) when the
   model's response has no plain-text content alongside the tool-call
   blocks — `handle_tool_calls` computes this before logging the response,
   independent of whether any actual reasoning text exists.

## Design decisions to make explicitly

- **Ruby's `logger: Logger.new` default-argument-value is evaluated once
  per call** (a fresh `Logger` is constructed for every `Agent.new` that
  doesn't pass one explicitly — Ruby re-evaluates keyword-argument default
  expressions at each call site, unlike Python's notorious "mutable
  default evaluated once at def-time" trap). Recommend `logger: Logger |
  None = None` in Python's `Agent.__init__` signature, with `self._logger =
  logger if logger is not None else Logger()` in the body — the classic,
  correct Python idiom for "a fresh instance per call," not `logger:
  Logger = Logger()` in the signature itself (which would share **one**
  `Logger` instance, and one open file handle, across every `Agent` that
  doesn't pass its own).
- **Module-level mutable state (`@quiet`, `@debug`, `@config`) needs a home
  that avoids a circular import between `__init__.py` and `logger.py`.**
  Ruby's `module Boukensha; @quiet = false; ...; end` at the top of
  `boukensha.rb` works because Ruby modules are just objects — `logger.rb`
  (required afterward) can freely call `Boukensha.debug?`/`Boukensha.config`
  at runtime. Python's `src/boukensha/__init__.py` is the natural place for
  the equivalent module-level `_quiet`, `_debug`, `_config` globals plus
  `quiet()`/`loud()`/`is_quiet()`/`debug()`/`is_debug()`/`get_config()`
  functions — but `__init__.py` also needs `from .logger import Logger` for
  re-export, and `logger.py` needs to call `is_debug()`/`get_config()`.
  Recommend `logger.py` do `import boukensha as _boukensha` **inside the
  method bodies** that need it (`raw`, `default_dir`), not at module
  top-level, which defers the lookup until after `boukensha/__init__.py`
  has finished executing and sidesteps the circular-import-at-import-time
  problem entirely. (Alternative considered and rejected: a separate
  `state.py` module — rejected because it has no Ruby equivalent to mirror
  and would be an invented abstraction not present in the source.)
- **CamelCase→snake_case for `provider_name`** — Ruby's
  `.gsub(/([a-z\d])([A-Z])/, '\1_\2').downcase` becomes Python
  `re.sub(r"([a-z0-9])([A-Z])", r"\1_\2", name).lower()`, applied to
  `type(backend).__name__` (Python's equivalent of Ruby's
  `backend.class.name.split("::").last`, which is already just the
  short class name — no `Boukensha::Backends::` prefix to strip in
  Python since there's no module-qualified class-name string).
- **`respond_to?(:usage_level)` → Python's `hasattr(backend,
  "usage_level")`,** same pattern already used for `Agent`'s
  `max_iterations` guard in `05_agent_loop`'s plan.
- **JSON line writes use `json.dumps(event, default=str)`, not bare
  `json.dumps`,** as a safety net — Ruby's `JSON.generate` on an
  arbitrary `Message`/tool-result value mostly works because those are
  already strings/hashes by the time they reach the logger, but recommend
  `default=str` defensively in Python in case a caller ever logs a
  non-JSON-serializable object (e.g. an exception instance) — a small,
  justified robustness addition, not a Ruby behavioural equivalent (Ruby's
  `JSON.generate` would raise `TypeError` on truly unserializable input
  too; this is a "nice to have" difference worth confirming rather than
  silently deciding).
- **Session-id generation uses `datetime.now(timezone.utc).strftime(...)`
  plus `secrets.token_hex(4)`,** the direct Python stdlib equivalents of
  Ruby's `Time.now.utc.strftime(...)` and `SecureRandom.hex(4)`.

## Testing

Ruby's `06_the_logger` has no automated tests, only `examples/example.rb`
(a real, network-driven run). Recommend:

1. Port `examples/example.py` 1:1 for manual smoke-testing against a real
   `.boukensha/` dir and a real API key — confirm a `.jsonl` file appears
   under `.boukensha/sessions/`.
2. Carry `05_agent_loop`'s test files forward; extend `test_config.py` to
   assert `mud_host`/etc. are genuinely absent this step (`hasattr(config,
   "mud_host")` is `False`), and extend `test_prompt_builder.py` to assert
   `builder.backend is the_backend_instance` (the new public attribute).
3. Add `tests/test_logger.py` covering, with a temp directory as `dir=`:
   - `session_start` is written immediately on construction, merging
     `snapshot=` into the event
   - each phase method (`iteration`, `limit_reached`, `turn_end`, `prompt`,
     `tool_call`, `tool_result`, `response`) writes exactly one correctly-
     shaped JSON line, in append mode (multiple calls → multiple lines)
   - `raw` writes nothing when `is_debug()` is `False`, writes when `True`
     (toggle via `boukensha.debug()` in the test, reset after)
   - `response`'s cost/usage computation: a fake backend with
     `estimate_cost`/`model`/`usage_unit` produces the expected
     `execution_metadata`; a backend without `estimate_cost` (or missing
     token counts) produces `cost_usd: None`, dropped by `.compact`-style
     filtering (i.e. the key is absent from the written JSON, not present
     with a `null` value) — confirm which of these two Ruby's `.compact`
     actually produces and match it exactly
   - `close()` closes the file handle (no further writes possible /
     no exception on double-close)
4. Extend `tests/test_agent.py` (carried from `05_agent_loop`) with:
   - a fake `logger` double asserting the right sequence of calls
     (`iteration` → `prompt` → `raw` → then either `tool_call`+
     `tool_result` pairs or `response`+`turn_end`) across a two-iteration
     run
   - a tool that raises inside its block → `tool_result` message content
     is `"ERROR: <ExceptionType>: <message>"`, `logger.tool_result` called
     with `ok=False, error=<message>`, and the loop **continues** (does not
     propagate the exception)

